<h2>2026-08-14 (follow-up) — Todo card with HTML in description nested the whole page, killing floating buttons + tab switch</h2>

<p><strong>Symptom (the recurring bug):</strong> on <code>/planning/daily</code>, when todo cards are shown the left-side floating buttons (Back to Top, Edit-with-AI, Chat) disappear and the top tab buttons stop switching tabs. A browser ancestor-chain probe proved the buttons were nested <em>inside</em> a todo card:</p>
<pre>body > div.container > div.tab-content > div.tab-content > div > div.mb-24
  > details.RemainingTodosPanel > div.RemainingTodosBody > div.todo-card
  > details.card > div.card-body > div.mb-2 > div.todo-card-pre > [button]</pre>
<p>That is the page-<strong>duplication</strong> signature: a full second copy of the page (nav, footer, floating buttons) was injected inside one card, collapsing the entire document into a single <code>&lt;details class="card"&gt;</code>. The real body-level buttons were clobbered (<code>#back-to-top NOT in DOM</code>) and tab clicks hit the buried duplicate.</p>

<h3>Root cause</h3>
<ul>
    <li><code>root/todo/todo_card.tt</code> emitted <code>[% tc.description %]</code> and <code>[% tc.comments %]</code> <strong>without the <code>| html</code> escape filter</strong> inside <code>div.todo-card-pre</code>. Any todo whose <code>description</code> (or <code>comments</code>) is itself a full HTML document gets inserted verbatim, nesting the entire page layout inside the card.</li>
    <li>This was dormant until the Focus Queue fix above: <code>@remaining_open_todos</code> was previously always empty, so the <code>RemainingTodosPanel</code> (where the offending card lived) never rendered. Populating the queue exposed the pre-existing template defect.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fix</h3>
<ul>
    <li><code>root/todo/todo_card.tt</code> — both <code>tc.description</code> and <code>tc.comments</code> now use <code>[% tc.description | html %]</code> / <code>[% tc.comments | html %]</code>. Description text is shown as text, never executed as markup. This protects <em>every</em> card everywhere (todo list, day/week/month, project pages), not just the planning page.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Verification:</strong> rendered <code>todo/todo_card.tt</code> with a todo whose <code>description</code> is a full <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;…&lt;nav&gt;…&lt;button class="ai2-float-btn"&gt;…</code> document. After the fix: real <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> = 0, real <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE&gt;</code> = 0, nested <code>ai2-float-btn</code> button = 0, and the literal markup is present only as escaped <code>&amp;lt;!DOCTYPE html&amp;gt;</code>. The card can no longer nest the page.</p>

<p><strong>Security note:</strong> this was an HTML-injection (stored-XSS-class) defect — a todo description containing attacker-influenced markup would have executed in the viewer's session. Escaping is the correct hardening regardless of the layout symptom.</p>

<hr>

<h2>2026-08-14 (follow-up 2) — Tab switching breaks on Back/Forward (anchor/hash desync)</h2>

<p><strong>Symptom:</strong> on <code>/planning/daily</code>, clicking a tab (Priorities ⇄ Master Plan ⇄ Calendar) shows it correctly, but using the browser <strong>Back button</strong> to return left <em>no tab visible</em> — the page went blank. Identified as a hash/anchor desync, not a CSS or markup error.</p>

<h3>Root cause</h3>
<ul>
    <li><code>switchTab()</code> in <code>root/static/js/planning/daily-plan-utils.js</code> called <code>history.pushState('#'+tabName)</code> on every click, but the script only handled the hash on the initial <code>load</code> event. There was <strong>no <code>popstate</code>/<code>hashchange</code> listener</strong>.</li>
    <li>So: a tab <em>click</em> both shows the panel AND updates the URL hash → works. But browser <strong>Back</strong> reverts the hash (e.g. <code>#calendar</code> → <code>#priorities</code>) with no click, so nothing re-applied <code>.active</code> to any <code>.tab-content</code> → every panel stayed <code>display:none</code> → blank page.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fix</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Extracted the show/hide into a pure <code>_activateTab(name)</code> helper (removes <code>active</code> from all <code>.tab-content</code>/<code>.tab-button</code>, adds it to the target + matching <code>[data-tab]</code> buttons by selector — robust to non-click callers).</li>
    <li><code>switchTab()</code> now calls <code>_activateTab()</code> then <code>pushState()</code> (unchanged click behaviour).</li>
    <li>Added <code>popstate</code> + <code>hashchange</code> listeners that call <code>_activateTab(location.hash)</code> <strong>without</strong> pushing a new history entry (no duplicate entries / no loop). Now Back/Forward re-shows the correct tab.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Verification:</strong> <code>node --check root/static/js/planning/daily-plan-utils.js</code> → parse OK. Static JS is served straight from disk (no restart needed). Hard-refresh <code>/planning/daily</code>, click each tab, then press Back/Forward — the correct panel should now always be visible.</p>
